{"bill":{"id":"119-s-629","congress":119,"billType":"s","billNumber":"629","title":"Emergency Conservation Program Improvement Act of 2025","introducedDate":"2025-02-19","latestActionDate":"2026-07-12","latestActionText":"Became Public Law No: 119-102.","policyArea":"Agriculture and Food","subjects":["Agricultural prices, subsidies, credit","Emergency planning and evacuation","Farmland","Fires","Forests, forestry, trees","Natural disasters"],"sponsorBioguideId":"F000463","cosponsorBioguideIds":["L000570","S001150"],"committeeCodes":[],"committeeNames":[],"officialSummary":"Emergency Conservation Program Improvement Act of 2025 This bill revises the Emergency Conservation Program (ECP) and the Emergency Forest Restoration Program (EFRP) to expand eligibility for payments to agricultural producers and owners of forest land impacted by natural disasters. The bill also provides additional options to receive an advance on cost-sharing payments before carrying out emergency measures. The bill expands advance ECP payments to include the replacement, repair, or restoration of farmland or conservation structures requiring an immediate response. Producers may receive an advance on cost-sharing payments for 75% of the cost of the replacement and 50% of the cost of the repair or restoration. Current law limits advance payments to 25% of the cost of the repair or replacement of fencing. Under EFRP, the bill allows owners of nonindustrial private forest land impacted by a natural disaster to receive an advance on cost-sharing payments for up to 75% of the cost of the emergency measures. Currently, advance payments are not available under the program. ECP and EFRP recipients must use the funds within 180 days after the funds are disbursed. This provides additional time to ECP recipients who currently must use the funds within 60 days. The bill also expands eligibility for payments under the programs to include emergency measures to address damages caused by (1) a wildfire that is not caused naturally, if the damage is caused by the spread of the wildfire due to natural causes; and (2) a wildfire that is caused by the federal government.","congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/senate-bill/629","source":"congress","sourceUrl":"https://www.govinfo.gov/bulkdata/BILLSTATUS/119/s/BILLSTATUS-119s629.xml","fetchedAt":"2026-07-29T01:29:56.555Z"},"classification":{"billId":"119-s-629","plainSummary":"This bill expands two federal programs that help farmers and forest landowners recover from natural disasters. It increases the percentage of costs that can be paid upfront before work begins, extends the time recipients have to use the funds from 60 to 180 days, and makes landowners eligible for assistance when damage from human-caused wildfires spreads due to natural conditions or when federal actions cause the fire. The changes apply to the Emergency Conservation Program and the Emergency Forest Restoration Program.","industries":[{"industry":"agriculture","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"The bill directly expands cost-sharing payments and advance funding for agricultural producers repairing farmland and conservation structures after disasters."},{"industry":"insurance","confidence":0.4,"rationale":"Expanded federal disaster assistance may affect demand for private agricultural and forestry insurance products."}],"method":"llm","model":"claude-sonnet-4-5","inputHash":"5de0148911360b19ed99bff9","classifiedAt":"2026-07-30T02:30:43.281Z"},"plain":{"whatItDoes":"This bill would change the Emergency Conservation Program and the Emergency Forest Restoration Program to expand eligibility for payments to farmers and owners of forest land impacted by natural disasters.","whoItTouches":"Farmers, and anyone who buys food.","everydayEffect":"If it passed, the change would show up in farm income and food prices. Nothing has changed yet. Most bills never become law.","confidence":"crs-summary","source":"Reworded from the official summary. St